    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c2553365_57a26206",
        "filename": "src/gpu/graphite/DrawPass.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 447,
      "author": {
        "id": 13824
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-09-12T19:38:06Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think we\u0027d want to use either requiredUniformBufferAlignment or requiredStorageBufferAlignment here depending on whether storageBufferPreferred() is true.",
      "revId": "9409546712a04e2e2963ced407519f92a9850c6a",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "0f119ed6_5658050e",
        "filename": "src/gpu/graphite/DrawPass.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 447,
      "author": {
        "id": 8341
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-09-12T21:03:41Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We could make two gatherers, one for the paint uniforms and one for the renderstep uniforms. The current plan is that paints use ssbo\u0027s when possible, and renderstep\u0027s will always use uniform buffers for their bespoke uniforms. The common draw data will go into ssbos under the same cases that the paint data goes into ssbos.\n\nAlternatively, we use the max alignment requirement. But this got me thinking about the impacts of this approach (which was how I\u0027d imagined we\u0027d do it because I liked the idea of data and size being safe for use in arrays regardless of alignment requirements).\n\nOn Intel that requires 256 byte alignment for uniform bindings, this triggers because we pack the data as if it could be an array of Foo\u0027s for SSBO use and assume the buffer binding\u0027s alignment is \u003c\u003d the uniform component alignment. When we can\u0027t use SSBOs and we always have to bind, we\u0027re always going to use at least 256 bytes per uniform block. But assuming intel supports storage buffers, does it allow accessing SSBO elements that have their element alignment \u003c 256 so long as the start of the buffer is preserved? Otherwise we may get out of sync with how the GPU accesses a Foo* array, since we will have padded between elements and it will not have.",
      "parentUuid": "c2553365_57a26206",
      "revId": "9409546712a04e2e2963ced407519f92a9850c6a",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7d7ae3a8_4a2b51ee",
        "filename": "src/gpu/graphite/DrawPass.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 447,
      "author": {
        "id": 5065
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-09-12T21:38:17Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I seem to recall that there was a reason to keep one gatherer but I can\u0027t remember what it is. But I agree that if we use the UniformManager to manage the block size we\u0027ll need two since renderstep and paint uniforms might have different alignments.\n\nI think Intel should support SSBOs since Metal in general does, but not sure on the access question. The layout of the data in memory should be the same, I\u0027d think. Where we put the data in the buffer should be how it looks on the GPU.",
      "parentUuid": "c2553365_57a26206",
      "revId": "9409546712a04e2e2963ced407519f92a9850c6a",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
